1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,100
He is not sick.

2
00:00:02,169 --> 00:00:04,899
Dr. Riley is throwing up.
He obviously can't lecture.

3
00:00:04,972 --> 00:00:07,964
You witnessed the spew?
Or you just have his word for it?

4
00:00:08,041 --> 00:00:11,670
I think I'm coming down
with a little bit of the clap.
I may have to go home for a few days.

5
00:00:11,745 --> 00:00:13,838
Dr. Riley doesn't have a history
of lying to me.

6
00:00:13,914 --> 00:00:16,849
You said this is the fifth time
he's missed a class this year.

7
00:00:16,917 --> 00:00:20,353
- Either he's dying or he's lying.
- I'll give you two hours off clinic duty.

8
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:23,253
Fine. I'll have Cameron do it.
She loves inspiring the inspired.

9
00:00:23,323 --> 00:00:26,520
- You'll do it.
- Why is it always me?

10
00:00:26,593 --> 00:00:29,528
Because the world hates you.
Or because it's a class on diagnostics.

11
00:00:29,596 --> 00:00:32,087
Pick whichever reason
feeds your narcissism better.

12
00:00:32,165 --> 00:00:34,190
I'm not doing it.

13
00:00:47,114 --> 00:00:49,344
You're supposed to stop me.
Renegotiate.

14
00:00:49,416 --> 00:00:51,646
And you were supposed
to keep on walking.

15
00:00:51,718 --> 00:00:54,278
Sorry. Guess we both screwed up.
Go on. Do it again.

16
00:00:55,822 --> 00:00:58,052
I'll do the lecture
for four hours off clinic.

17
00:00:58,125 --> 00:01:01,356
Two. I know you'd rather
spend a couple of hours...

18
00:01:01,428 --> 00:01:04,124
listening to yourself
than listening to patients.

19
00:01:04,197 --> 00:01:07,064
- [Sighs]
- Class starts in 20 minutes.

20
00:01:10,203 --> 00:01:13,832
- Dr. House, there's a patient.
- I'm outta here. Take it up with Cuddy.

21
00:01:13,907 --> 00:01:15,841
[Woman]
Greg.

22
00:01:21,515 --> 00:01:23,483
Hi, Stacy.

23
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:26,018
How ya doin'?

24
00:01:26,086 --> 00:01:28,646
How am I doing?

25
00:01:29,856 --> 00:01:32,586
The last five years have been like-

26
00:01:32,659 --> 00:01:35,423
You ever see those
Girls Gone Wild videos?

27
00:01:35,495 --> 00:01:39,056
Your life's been like that?
Or your life's been spent watching them?

28
00:01:43,937 --> 00:01:45,996
I have missed you.

29
00:01:47,307 --> 00:01:49,434
Is that why you're here?

30
00:01:49,509 --> 00:01:51,841
[Sighs]

31
00:01:54,247 --> 00:01:57,444
I need your help.

32
00:02:06,126 --> 00:02:09,960
Who am I looking at?
My husband.

33
00:02:12,599 --> 00:02:17,195
Who is suffering abdominal pain
and fainting spells.

34
00:02:17,270 --> 00:02:20,967
No sign of tumors, no vasculitis.

35
00:02:21,041 --> 00:02:23,805
Could be indigestion.

36
00:02:23,877 --> 00:02:28,974
Or maybe a kidney stone.
Little one can pack a lot of wallop.

37
00:02:29,049 --> 00:02:30,949
Did you think I wasn't
going to get married?

38
00:02:31,017 --> 00:02:34,851
[Sighs]
Not to someone so poorly endowed.

39
00:02:34,921 --> 00:02:37,151
This guy's pancreas is pathetic.

40
00:02:41,695 --> 00:02:44,061
There is no kidney stone,
no indigestion.

41
00:02:44,131 --> 00:02:47,567
Three hospitals, five doctors-
Not one of them found anything.

42
00:02:47,634 --> 00:02:49,625
Maybe there's nothing to be found.

43
00:02:49,703 --> 00:02:53,264
Right. You suddenly trust doctors,
love puppies and long walks in the rain.

44
00:02:53,340 --> 00:02:55,331
Walks are out.

45
00:02:55,409 --> 00:02:58,776
I was around you long enough
to know when something's not right.

46
00:02:58,845 --> 00:03:02,713
Mark's had personality changes.
He's acting strange, disconnected.

47
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:04,717
Interesting.

48
00:03:04,785 --> 00:03:08,448
Means there's either a neurological
component, or he's having an affair.

49
00:03:08,522 --> 00:03:10,956
No affair. No nothing. He's sick.

50
00:03:12,726 --> 00:03:15,490
I know you're not too busy.
You avoid work like the plague.

51
00:03:15,562 --> 00:03:18,156
Unless it actually is the plague.

52
00:03:18,231 --> 00:03:22,167
I'm asking you a favor.
I'm not too busy.

53
00:03:24,271 --> 00:03:27,297
But I'm not sure I want him to live.

54
00:03:29,276 --> 00:03:31,335
It's good seeing you again.

55
00:04:23,163 --> 00:04:26,724
Three guys walk into a clinic.

56
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:30,497
Their legs hurt.
What's wrong with them?

57
00:04:33,006 --> 00:04:34,940
I'm not gonna like you, am I?

58
00:04:35,008 --> 00:04:38,102
The most likely cause of leg pain
is muscle strain.

59
00:04:38,178 --> 00:04:40,942
Apply heat
and rest affected areas.

60
00:04:41,014 --> 00:04:43,505
Statistically, you're right.
Very good.

61
00:04:43,583 --> 00:04:46,450
My experience, over half ofleg pain
is musculoskeletal,

62
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:48,647
generally from excessive exercise,

63
00:04:48,722 --> 00:04:52,590
Twelve percent is varicose veins
brought on by pregnancy,

64
00:04:52,659 --> 00:04:55,822
and most of the rest is the result
of vehicular accidents.

65
00:04:55,896 --> 00:04:58,865
I said three people.
That's six legs.

66
00:04:58,932 --> 00:05:03,562
So you've got three hurt jogging, two
in collisions, and one of the legs is pregnant.

67
00:05:03,637 --> 00:05:06,629
- [Light Chuckling]
- What were they doing when pain presented?

68
00:05:06,706 --> 00:05:10,164
- I have no idea.
- You didn't ask? You didn't take a history?

69
00:05:10,243 --> 00:05:14,543
Of course. But all that told us
is what they said happened.

70
00:05:14,614 --> 00:05:18,516
Person "A," farmer,
says he was fixing a fence.

71
00:05:18,585 --> 00:05:21,418
[Bird Squawking]

72
00:05:21,488 --> 00:05:25,754
Tightness in the ankle,
loss of muscle control.
[Groaning]

73
00:05:25,825 --> 00:05:29,090
Person "B," volleyball practice.

74
00:05:29,162 --> 00:05:31,858
[Indistinct Shouting]

75
00:05:32,966 --> 00:05:36,697
- [Groaning]
- Coach figured it was a pulled muscle.

76
00:05:36,770 --> 00:05:40,536
And "C," we've got Carmen Electra golfing.

77
00:05:41,575 --> 00:05:45,443
- Yes!
- Whoa. You treated the Baywatch chick?

78
00:05:45,512 --> 00:05:49,312
The Baywatch thespian.
And no.

79
00:05:49,382 --> 00:05:52,112
I gotta disguise the identity
of each of the patients,

80
00:05:52,185 --> 00:05:54,278
and I got tired
of using the middle-aged man.

81
00:05:54,354 --> 00:05:57,653
Carmen seemed like a pleasant alternative.

82
00:05:57,724 --> 00:05:59,749
Also, she's apparently quite the golfer.

83
00:05:59,826 --> 00:06:03,318
In less than two hours,
one of these three...

84
00:06:03,396 --> 00:06:06,923
will be tossed out of the hospital because
they were faking it to score narcotics,

85
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:11,903
and one will be very close to death.

86
00:06:11,972 --> 00:06:14,805
Any guesses on which is which?

87
00:06:16,343 --> 00:06:19,073
Okay, I say we start with the farmer.

88
00:06:20,547 --> 00:06:23,573
Did you hike to the fence,
and how far?

89
00:06:23,650 --> 00:06:27,643
Yes. It's about a half a mile
from my farmhouse.

90
00:06:27,721 --> 00:06:29,655
Where is the pain localized?

91
00:06:29,723 --> 00:06:32,886
It started just above my ankle,
and it's radiating up.

92
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:35,325
So, what should we do first?

93
00:06:35,395 --> 00:06:37,329
Family history?

94
00:06:37,397 --> 00:06:39,456
Indicative of leg pain?
That's a very short list.

95
00:06:39,532 --> 00:06:44,333
Any history of bone cancer, osteogenesis
imperfecta or multiple myeloma?

96
00:06:44,404 --> 00:06:46,304
Could be a blood issue.

97
00:06:46,373 --> 00:06:49,900
We should run a C.B.C.
And a D-Dimer.
And get an M.R.I.

98
00:06:49,976 --> 00:06:52,740
M.R.I. Or a PET scan?
If the problem's vascular,
he's better off-

99
00:06:52,812 --> 00:06:57,579
[Buzzes]
Sorry. Thanks for playin'.
Patient's dead. You killed him.

100
00:06:59,853 --> 00:07:01,980
We had no time to run any tests.
There was nothing we could do.

101
00:07:02,055 --> 00:07:04,615
You had time to look at the leg.

102
00:07:09,362 --> 00:07:11,626
I thought we were starting
with the farmer's case first.

103
00:07:11,698 --> 00:07:15,930
We are. But if we're
gonna look at a leg-

104
00:07:16,002 --> 00:07:18,061
I need you to take off your pants.

105
00:07:46,533 --> 00:07:48,694
Puncture.

106
00:07:48,768 --> 00:07:50,633
[Female Student]
Snakebite.
That would be my guess.

107
00:07:50,704 --> 00:07:53,070
Farmer didn't know
he'd been bitten by a snake?
That's what he said.

108
00:07:53,139 --> 00:07:56,734
Sudden shooting pain, tall grass.
Never saw a thing.

109
00:07:56,810 --> 00:08:00,974
- What kind of snake?
- You want me to tell you what kind of snake
it was from the shape of the hole in the leg?

110
00:08:01,047 --> 00:08:04,175
How are we to know
what kind of antivenom to use
if we don't know what kind of snake it is?

111
00:08:04,250 --> 00:08:07,185
Oh, there are people
to find those things out.

112
00:08:07,253 --> 00:08:09,949
Shouldn't we wait
for the Humane Society or something?

113
00:08:10,023 --> 00:08:13,686
- Guy might only have a couple of hours.
- [Dog Barking]

114
00:08:19,265 --> 00:08:22,928
And while we wait
for the Humane Society to show up,

115
00:08:23,002 --> 00:08:25,562
what say we check in
on the volleyball player.

116
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:29,400
You have tendinitis.

117
00:08:29,476 --> 00:08:31,535
[Female Student]
How old is this person?

118
00:08:31,611 --> 00:08:35,274
I mean, it's not really a 40-year-old man
on a girls' volleyball team, right?

119
00:08:35,348 --> 00:08:37,612
It's a leg.
A leg is a leg is a leg.

120
00:08:37,684 --> 00:08:41,085
- Well, I was just worried that-
- Would you worry about her more
if she was younger?

121
00:08:42,789 --> 00:08:46,054
Obviously, we should care about all
our patients, no matter what age-

122
00:08:46,126 --> 00:08:49,357
Yeah, right. I saw the way
you were looking at Carmen.

123
00:08:49,429 --> 00:08:52,023
She's mine. Stay away.

124
00:08:52,098 --> 00:08:56,501
- Would you operate on your mother?
- Of course not.

125
00:08:56,569 --> 00:08:59,129
I'd be too nervous.
Couldn't be objective.

126
00:08:59,205 --> 00:09:02,868
Then why are you so anxious
to treat every patient like they're family?

127
00:09:02,942 --> 00:09:06,139
The actual patient is 16.

128
00:09:06,212 --> 00:09:08,476
Here's what happens
when doctors care too much.

129
00:09:09,682 --> 00:09:12,082
I need to know everything about you.

130
00:09:14,187 --> 00:09:16,121
[Cameron]
I went back three generations.

131
00:09:16,189 --> 00:09:18,919
No history of cancer, Parkinson's
or any other degenerative condition.

132
00:09:18,992 --> 00:09:21,483
But there's this boy at school,
and he's on the boys' volleyball team.

133
00:09:21,561 --> 00:09:23,722
They made out at a party,
and now he won't call her back.

134
00:09:23,797 --> 00:09:27,563
And this friend of hers at school
said this boy didn't like her
and never did.

135
00:09:27,634 --> 00:09:29,898
You got all this
from an examination of the knee?

136
00:09:29,969 --> 00:09:32,164
I think she's depressed.
She doesn't have tendinitis?

137
00:09:32,238 --> 00:09:34,399
She has tendinitis.
She's depressed
about having tendinitis?

138
00:09:34,474 --> 00:09:36,738
She's depressed for the same
reason that she has tendinitis.
Not the boy?

139
00:09:36,810 --> 00:09:39,973
No, the boy's a jerk. She knows that,
and yet she's depressed.

140
00:09:40,046 --> 00:09:42,139
I found a nodule.

141
00:09:42,215 --> 00:09:46,379
Ahh. Problems with the thyroid gland-
causes depressed mental state...

142
00:09:46,452 --> 00:09:48,750
can cause inflammation
of the tendons.

143
00:09:48,822 --> 00:09:51,290
I'll run the tests.

144
00:09:51,357 --> 00:09:54,087
[Female Student]
So because she took
such an extreme interest,

145
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:56,651
she found out that the person
had a thyroid condition.

146
00:09:56,729 --> 00:10:01,132
No. Because she took such an interest,
she discovered a tiny nodule...

147
00:10:01,201 --> 00:10:05,262
which in reality signified nothing
but gave us no choice...

148
00:10:05,338 --> 00:10:09,468
but to put a person with tendinitis
through an expensive and painful test.

149
00:10:14,080 --> 00:10:16,014
[Gasps]

150
00:10:16,082 --> 00:10:19,279
Here's how a well-adjusted
doctor handles a case.

151
00:10:25,625 --> 00:10:29,322
Can I put my pants back on now?
I'd rather you didn't.

152
00:10:29,395 --> 00:10:31,886
Which Carmen Electra is this?

153
00:10:31,965 --> 00:10:34,331
The first one. The golfer.

154
00:10:34,400 --> 00:10:37,494
Then why isn't she wearing pants?

155
00:10:37,570 --> 00:10:41,370
You have decreased reflexes
in your patellar tendon.

156
00:10:41,441 --> 00:10:43,739
- Anyone?
- Slipped disc.

157
00:10:43,810 --> 00:10:46,643
Could be. How bad does it hurt?

158
00:10:46,713 --> 00:10:50,205
- It hurts really, really bad.
- Yeah?

159
00:10:50,283 --> 00:10:52,513
It doesn't seem real.
Is she the one faking?

160
00:10:52,585 --> 00:10:57,079
Oh, for God's sake.
She's here to play into my fantasy,
not 'cause she's Meryl Streep.

161
00:11:00,226 --> 00:11:01,887
Fine.

162
00:11:01,961 --> 00:11:04,987
What the hell is wrong with me!

163
00:11:05,064 --> 00:11:07,897
Do something!
[Groaning]

164
00:11:07,967 --> 00:11:11,733
Too much pain to be a slipped disc.
Could be herniated
and impinging the nerve root.

165
00:11:11,804 --> 00:11:14,034
Or it could be referred pain
from his groin.

166
00:11:14,107 --> 00:11:18,043
Sir, are you getting pain
anywhere else?
[Screaming]

167
00:11:18,111 --> 00:11:21,603
- He's curling. It's not the back.
- Sir, where are you getting pain?

168
00:11:21,681 --> 00:11:25,344
- Help me.!
- He's not gonna tell us anything
if we don't get him out of pain.

169
00:11:25,418 --> 00:11:28,751
- Give him 50 milligrams of Demerol.
- We have no history. He could be allergic.

170
00:11:28,821 --> 00:11:31,585
- What do I do?
- We can't diagnose him while he screams.

171
00:11:31,658 --> 00:11:34,286
Better than killing him
with painkillers. That-

172
00:11:34,360 --> 00:11:38,194
[Grunts, Groaning]

173
00:11:39,799 --> 00:11:41,994
[Sighs]

174
00:11:42,068 --> 00:11:44,696
Apparently he's not allergic.

175
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:50,373
Thank you. I feel a lot better now.

176
00:11:54,013 --> 00:11:55,947
We screwed up.

177
00:11:56,015 --> 00:11:58,950
No. You did exactly
what his attending did.

178
00:11:59,018 --> 00:12:01,486
And that was the proper way
to handle the case?
Yeah.

179
00:12:01,554 --> 00:12:03,579
The guy used him as a dealer.

180
00:12:03,656 --> 00:12:06,454
You're gonna see a lot of drug-seeking
behavior in your practice.

181
00:12:06,526 --> 00:12:08,460
There's a reason- It works.

182
00:12:08,528 --> 00:12:12,294
Meanwhile, back on the farm-

183
00:12:12,365 --> 00:12:15,334
[Rattling]

184
00:12:16,903 --> 00:12:19,633
Yeah. Timber rattlesnake.

185
00:12:19,706 --> 00:12:23,335
Four vials of the CroFab antivenom.

186
00:12:23,409 --> 00:12:25,604
- Hey, how ya doin'?
- All right.

187
00:12:25,678 --> 00:12:30,138
Thank you. This will start making you
feel better really fast.

188
00:12:33,853 --> 00:12:38,017
[Gasps, Wheezing]

189
00:12:38,091 --> 00:12:41,424
He's having an allergic reaction. Bag.
[Wheezing Continues]

190
00:12:43,563 --> 00:12:45,190
Paddles and epi.

191
00:12:45,264 --> 00:12:47,596
His heart's fine.
It's not gonna stay that way.

192
00:12:47,667 --> 00:12:50,830
- [Alarm Beeping]
- Paddles!

193
00:12:57,577 --> 00:12:59,636
What say we take five,

194
00:12:59,712 --> 00:13:02,476
get some coffee, go pee.

195
00:13:10,590 --> 00:13:14,151
You didn't think
she was gonna get married?

196
00:13:14,227 --> 00:13:16,787
She asked me the same question.

197
00:13:19,232 --> 00:13:22,429
And... what?
You're not gonna treat him?

198
00:13:25,004 --> 00:13:27,564
There's probably nothing wrong with him.

199
00:13:27,640 --> 00:13:31,235
Oh, sure, that makes sense.
She's just using the old
sick husband routine...

200
00:13:31,310 --> 00:13:33,676
as an excuse to get
back in touch with you.

201
00:13:33,746 --> 00:13:36,214
You think this is easy for her?

202
00:13:36,282 --> 00:13:39,217
The only reason she'd be anywhere
near you is if she was desperate.

203
00:13:39,285 --> 00:13:41,913
So I should help her
because she hates me?

204
00:13:41,988 --> 00:13:45,116
She doesn't hate you.
She loves you.

205
00:13:45,191 --> 00:13:50,925
She just... can't stand
to be around you.

206
00:13:53,666 --> 00:13:55,600
Uh, Dr. House?

207
00:13:55,668 --> 00:13:58,193
It's been almost six minutes.

208
00:13:59,639 --> 00:14:01,573
[Sighs]
[Taps Cane]

209
00:14:06,145 --> 00:14:08,079
Found him.

210
00:14:09,482 --> 00:14:11,473
The volleyball player...

211
00:14:13,152 --> 00:14:18,454
was responding to the anti-inflammatories
as you'd expect in a case of tendinitis.

212
00:14:18,524 --> 00:14:21,220
Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa.
What about the snakebite guy?

213
00:14:21,294 --> 00:14:23,660
I don't really care
about the volleyball player.

214
00:14:23,729 --> 00:14:26,994
What if I told you the volleyball player
had a sudden massive stroke?

215
00:14:27,066 --> 00:14:29,899
- Really?
- No.

216
00:14:29,969 --> 00:14:33,598
But that would make you interested, right?
What if her T-4 came back low?

217
00:14:33,673 --> 00:14:36,836
It's not quite as interesting,
but it has the benefit ofbeing true.

218
00:14:36,909 --> 00:14:39,639
You said the thyroid biopsy
was a wasted test.

219
00:14:39,712 --> 00:14:44,809
No, I didn't. I said
she put a person with tendinitis
through an expensive and painful test.

220
00:14:44,884 --> 00:14:48,411
Apparently, the patient had tendinitis
and a thyroid condition.

221
00:14:48,488 --> 00:14:52,549
We're gonna start you on thyroxine.
It'll make you feel better
and level your moods.

222
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:56,694
Thank you.

223
00:15:06,506 --> 00:15:10,272
- So that's it?
- You were right the first time.

224
00:15:10,343 --> 00:15:14,837
Snakebite guy's way more interesting.
Gross, actually.

225
00:15:17,483 --> 00:15:21,613
[Foreman]
The patient responded to epinephrine,
and the allergic reaction was arrested.

226
00:15:21,687 --> 00:15:24,212
Unfortunately, the patient
continues to deteriorate.

227
00:15:24,290 --> 00:15:27,191
Maybe the snake wrangler
was wrong about the type we caught.

228
00:15:27,260 --> 00:15:31,890
He faxed us the venom test.
Confirmed. It's a timber rattlesnake.

229
00:15:31,964 --> 00:15:34,797
No, it's not. Notice the volume?

230
00:15:34,867 --> 00:15:39,361
I skimmed over that
and the gender and the coloring.
Jumped right to the name of the snake.

231
00:15:39,438 --> 00:15:44,171
Two hundred milligrams.
Our guy got bit less than four hours ago.

232
00:15:44,243 --> 00:15:47,906
There's no way a snake regenerates
that much venom that quickly.

233
00:15:47,980 --> 00:15:51,143
- We're supposed to know how fast
snakes make their venom?
- No.

234
00:15:51,217 --> 00:15:56,177
Unless you've got a patient bit by one.
Then it might be helpful.
So what do we do now?

235
00:15:56,255 --> 00:16:00,692
He must have been bitten by a different
snake. We go back and find it.

236
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:03,661
Or you go online, and you find
there's only three poisonous snakes
common in New Jersey:

237
00:16:03,729 --> 00:16:06,197
The copperhead, timber rattler
and the coral.

238
00:16:06,265 --> 00:16:10,258
Copperhead and timber rattler both respond
to the antivenoms we gave the guy.

239
00:16:10,336 --> 00:16:14,568
- So we give him the antivenom
for the other one?
- Is that a question?

240
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:18,542
We can't just blindly give him
another antivenom, especially
after the first one almost killed him.

241
00:16:18,611 --> 00:16:22,172
You said only three types of poisonous
snakes commonly found in New Jersey.

242
00:16:22,248 --> 00:16:25,445
- Well, what if this is an uncommon one?
- Very good.

243
00:16:25,518 --> 00:16:28,078
- We gotta find the right snake.
- No need.

244
00:16:28,154 --> 00:16:31,851
Odds are by the time you get back,
the autopsy results will tell you
what kind of snake it was.

245
00:16:31,924 --> 00:16:35,087
- But you said-
- So we do give him the antivenom
for the other one?

246
00:16:35,161 --> 00:16:39,791
Again, was that a question?
I asked what you would do.

247
00:16:39,865 --> 00:16:42,561
It seems unfair for you to ask me
what you would do.

248
00:16:42,635 --> 00:16:45,832
Who gives the guy
the other antivenom?

249
00:16:46,973 --> 00:16:49,066
And who goes looking for the snake?

250
00:16:51,344 --> 00:16:54,313
I assume that one choice kills him,
the other one saves him.

251
00:16:54,380 --> 00:16:57,213
That's usually the way it works
at the leg-turning-black stage.

252
00:16:57,283 --> 00:17:01,242
So half of us killed him,
and half of us saved his life.

253
00:17:01,320 --> 00:17:05,120
- Yeah.
- We can't be blamed for-

254
00:17:05,191 --> 00:17:10,151
I'm sure this goes against
everything you've been taught,
but right and wrong do exist.

255
00:17:10,229 --> 00:17:12,288
Just because you don't know
what the right answer is-

256
00:17:12,365 --> 00:17:15,061
maybe there's even no way you
could know what the right answer is-

257
00:17:15,134 --> 00:17:17,568
doesn't make your answer right
or even okay.

258
00:17:17,637 --> 00:17:22,006
It's much simpler than that.
It's just plain wrong.

259
00:17:24,710 --> 00:17:28,146
[House]
We gave the guy the antivenom.
What if I'm allergic again?

260
00:17:28,214 --> 00:17:30,478
That's why these people are here.

261
00:17:30,549 --> 00:17:34,178
If you have a reaction,
we're ready to do whatever's necessary...

262
00:17:34,253 --> 00:17:37,154
to ensure your airways stay open
and your heart keeps beating.

263
00:17:37,223 --> 00:17:39,953
My wife's on her way in.
Can't this wait?

264
00:17:40,026 --> 00:17:43,189
I'm sorry. It can't.

265
00:18:12,658 --> 00:18:16,355
It hurts again.
[Female Student]
He came back?

266
00:18:16,429 --> 00:18:19,489
On average,
drug addicts are stupid.

267
00:18:21,467 --> 00:18:24,061
- I'd call the cops.
- Good for you.

268
00:18:24,136 --> 00:18:26,969
A lot of doctors wouldn't risk
their careers on a hunch.

269
00:18:27,039 --> 00:18:29,667
It's not a hunch.
I mean, I know he wants drugs.

270
00:18:29,742 --> 00:18:31,869
Well, even drug addicts get sick.

271
00:18:31,944 --> 00:18:36,643
Actually, for some reason,
they tend to get sick more often
than non-drug addicts.

272
00:18:38,284 --> 00:18:41,742
Luckily, you don't
have to play your hunch.

273
00:18:41,821 --> 00:18:44,346
There's a faster way.
Actually, there are several.

274
00:18:44,423 --> 00:18:46,584
My preference is urine testing.

275
00:18:46,659 --> 00:18:50,493
- But you already know
he has drugs in his system.
- That's not what I'm testing for.

276
00:18:50,563 --> 00:18:55,159
We're gonna put this hard rubber tube
up your urethra and into your bladder.

277
00:18:55,234 --> 00:18:59,500
- It may be a little uncomfortable.
- Shouldn't I be getting
some sort of anesthetic?

278
00:18:59,572 --> 00:19:02,040
[Groaning]
We're concerned
about allergic reactions today.

279
00:19:02,108 --> 00:19:05,168
[Groaning Continues]

280
00:19:10,149 --> 00:19:14,916
If the guy can handle a rod in his penis
for half an hour, he's really sick.

281
00:19:14,987 --> 00:19:17,046
Or he's really jonesing.

282
00:19:17,123 --> 00:19:19,284
There's easier ways
to get ahold of drugs.

283
00:19:19,358 --> 00:19:21,792
Other hospitals, for example.
[Sniffs]

284
00:19:23,129 --> 00:19:26,223
The volleyball player
is not responding to treatment.

285
00:19:29,535 --> 00:19:31,935
At least we think it's not working...

286
00:19:32,004 --> 00:19:34,268
on account of the fact
that she's getting worse.

287
00:19:34,340 --> 00:19:37,138
- [Shouting] Can you still hear me?
- No.

288
00:19:37,209 --> 00:19:39,143
A little!
Not really!

289
00:19:41,213 --> 00:19:43,943
[Quietly]
Well, if you can't hear me,
how do you know what I asked?

290
00:19:44,016 --> 00:19:46,382
- Ow!
- I'm sorry. What did I do?

291
00:19:46,452 --> 00:19:50,718
I don't know.
It really hurts.
I promise to be very careful.

292
00:19:50,790 --> 00:19:53,452
- Susan-
- Not her real name.

293
00:19:53,526 --> 00:19:57,326
- Susan, I barely touched you.
- [Screaming]

294
00:19:57,396 --> 00:20:01,457
It hurts so much!
I'm sorry. I have to get
this blood. Just hold on.

295
00:20:01,534 --> 00:20:03,297
Hypersensitivity to touch.

296
00:20:03,369 --> 00:20:05,894
- [Chase] Her calcium up?
- Lab over 16.

297
00:20:05,971 --> 00:20:09,634
- Question is why. Likely suspects.
- Parathyroid adenoma.

298
00:20:09,708 --> 00:20:11,801
- Kidney problems.
- Vit "D" intoxication.
- Hyperthyroid.

299
00:20:11,877 --> 00:20:13,435
- Caused by our treatment?
- Whoa, whoa.

300
00:20:13,512 --> 00:20:15,173
Can you please slow down?

301
00:20:16,248 --> 00:20:19,183
[Spits Out Water]

302
00:20:19,251 --> 00:20:22,948
The adenoma is most likely.

303
00:20:23,022 --> 00:20:26,355
Check her P. T.H., phosphorus...

304
00:20:26,425 --> 00:20:29,087
and ionized calcium.

305
00:20:29,161 --> 00:20:31,356
And do a technetium sestamibi.

306
00:20:31,430 --> 00:20:35,093
Okay, that's enough
about the volleyball player.

307
00:20:35,167 --> 00:20:38,466
- What's up with the farmer?
- What farmer?

308
00:20:38,537 --> 00:20:40,437
Snakebite guy.

309
00:20:40,506 --> 00:20:43,270
Oh, right. You guys
don't know about him.

310
00:20:43,342 --> 00:20:47,108
He doesn't get bitten until three months
after we treat the volleyball player.

311
00:20:47,179 --> 00:20:50,478
Luckily, it's been well established
that time is not a fixed construct.

312
00:20:50,549 --> 00:20:53,985
His condition is not improving.
Double the dosage.

313
00:20:54,053 --> 00:20:56,385
- Already did.
- There's another antivenom.

314
00:20:56,455 --> 00:20:58,514
- It's not as effective, but-
- Already tried it.

315
00:20:58,591 --> 00:21:01,617
- The first stuff, the stuff he was allergic to.
- Gave it to him with high-dose steroids.

316
00:21:01,694 --> 00:21:04,720
- Nothing's working.
- What does it all mean?

317
00:21:04,797 --> 00:21:08,563
- Wrong snake?
- We tried every other antivenom we had.

318
00:21:08,634 --> 00:21:13,037
- We're too late?
- Yep. He's dying. His wife's here.

319
00:21:13,105 --> 00:21:16,836
Finally found a babysitter.
Who wants to let 'em know?

320
00:21:18,577 --> 00:21:21,011
- Actually, I'm kidding.
- He's not dying?

321
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:24,379
Oh, yeah, he's dying.
But there's no wife and kid.

322
00:21:24,450 --> 00:21:26,384
Which is great.

323
00:21:26,452 --> 00:21:30,411
Makes the breaking-the-news
thing way easier.

324
00:21:30,489 --> 00:21:32,855
Oh, yeah.
One more piece of news.

325
00:21:35,828 --> 00:21:38,126
[Groaning]

326
00:21:38,197 --> 00:21:42,429
The drug addict is peeing blood.

327
00:21:46,672 --> 00:21:50,108
How do they teach you how
to tell someone that they're dying?

328
00:21:54,046 --> 00:21:58,676
It's kind of like teaching architects
how to explain why their building fell down.

329
00:21:58,751 --> 00:22:00,981
Do you role-play and stuff?

330
00:22:01,053 --> 00:22:05,490
Yeah. One of us gives the bad news,
and one of us gets the bad news.

331
00:22:05,558 --> 00:22:09,050
What do you have to do to get an "A"
in You're Dying 101?

332
00:22:09,128 --> 00:22:12,859
They grade you on gentleness
and supportiveness?

333
00:22:12,932 --> 00:22:17,426
Is there a scale
for measuring compassion?

334
00:22:18,737 --> 00:22:20,671
This buddy of mine-

335
00:22:20,739 --> 00:22:24,197
I gotta give him 10 bucks
every time someone says thank you.

336
00:22:24,276 --> 00:22:28,076
Imagine that.
This guy's so good,

337
00:22:28,147 --> 00:22:31,708
people thank him for telling 'em
that they're dying.

338
00:22:36,088 --> 00:22:38,079
Needs brown.

339
00:22:38,157 --> 00:22:41,251
I don't get thanked that often.

340
00:22:41,327 --> 00:22:43,227
You're dying.

341
00:22:45,164 --> 00:22:47,257
In a few hours.

342
00:22:49,201 --> 00:22:52,432
There's nothing we can do
except deal with the pain.

343
00:22:52,504 --> 00:22:54,904
Well, I need to go home.

344
00:22:54,974 --> 00:22:56,908
You're not going home.

345
00:22:59,044 --> 00:23:03,743
But my dog.
What will happen to my dog?

346
00:23:09,254 --> 00:23:11,722
Her neck looks clean.
No adenoma.

347
00:23:11,790 --> 00:23:15,658
Wait, wait, wait. The guy's dying,
and all he cares about is his dog?

348
00:23:17,496 --> 00:23:21,956
[Crayon Scratching]
Any of you guys go the dog route
in your improv sessions?

349
00:23:23,869 --> 00:23:28,533
It's a basic truth of the human condition
that everybody lies.

350
00:23:29,808 --> 00:23:33,005
The only variable is about what.

351
00:23:33,078 --> 00:23:35,342
The great thing about telling
someone they're dying...

352
00:23:35,414 --> 00:23:38,679
is that it tends to focus their priorities.

353
00:23:38,751 --> 00:23:41,652
You find out what matters to them.

354
00:23:41,720 --> 00:23:44,712
What they're willing to die for.

355
00:23:46,859 --> 00:23:48,793
What they're willing to lie for.

356
00:23:48,861 --> 00:23:52,422
You must have a neighbor who-
Neighbors don't like him.

357
00:23:53,999 --> 00:23:57,833
So the Humane Society will
take him in and find him a home.
No, they won't.

358
00:23:59,772 --> 00:24:03,435
[Muttering]
Yeah, but maybe my aunt-

359
00:24:03,509 --> 00:24:05,500
It wasn't a snakebite, was it?

360
00:24:08,013 --> 00:24:09,947
I said I don't remember bein' bit.

361
00:24:10,015 --> 00:24:12,540
Sure you do. Just not by a snake.

362
00:24:12,618 --> 00:24:17,317
I assume that Cujo bit
one of your neighbors a while back.

363
00:24:17,389 --> 00:24:20,825
You tell me that he bit you,
and I gotta report it.

364
00:24:20,893 --> 00:24:23,487
Cujo's got a record.
He gets the chair.

365
00:24:26,899 --> 00:24:31,700
The good news is you might just live.
The bad news is your dog's gonna die.

366
00:24:31,770 --> 00:24:35,297
- [Barking]
- Guy risked his life to save this thing?

367
00:24:35,374 --> 00:24:37,672
[Grunting, Straining]

368
00:24:37,743 --> 00:24:40,871
I'll hold him.
You swab the mouth.

369
00:24:40,946 --> 00:24:44,507
I think I've got a better grip here.
You go for the mouth.

370
00:24:44,583 --> 00:24:47,177
[Barking Continues]
All right. I'm odds.

371
00:24:47,252 --> 00:24:49,720
You serious?
One, two, three!

372
00:24:49,788 --> 00:24:52,188
Damn.

373
00:24:52,257 --> 00:24:55,488
I don't care if he's scratching
your nads off, you don't let go.

374
00:24:55,561 --> 00:24:56,994
Just do it!

375
00:24:59,064 --> 00:25:01,498
I say we let the guy die.

376
00:25:01,567 --> 00:25:04,559
- I got his head. Just do it.
- Got it.

377
00:25:09,308 --> 00:25:12,175
What would you call that?

378
00:25:12,244 --> 00:25:15,407
It's tea-colored, right?

379
00:25:15,481 --> 00:25:19,212
The guy we thought
was just after the drugs-

380
00:25:19,284 --> 00:25:23,812
What's the differential diagnosis
for urine that's tea-colored?

381
00:25:23,889 --> 00:25:26,414
[Female Student]
Kidney stone.

382
00:25:26,492 --> 00:25:29,086
Kidney stones would cause what?

383
00:25:29,161 --> 00:25:31,857
- Blood in urine.
- What color is your pee?

384
00:25:31,930 --> 00:25:33,864
- Yellow.
- What color is your blood?

385
00:25:33,932 --> 00:25:35,957
- Red.
- What colors did I use?

386
00:25:36,034 --> 00:25:39,060
Red, yellow and brown.

387
00:25:39,138 --> 00:25:42,107
- And brown. What causes brown?
- Waste.

388
00:25:42,174 --> 00:25:44,608
- Which means the kidneys
are shutting down. Why?
- Trauma.

389
00:25:44,676 --> 00:25:48,305
- None that his history would indicate.
- Could be damage done
by the self-injection of the Demerol.

390
00:25:48,380 --> 00:25:50,541
- Treatment?
- Heat and rest-

391
00:25:50,616 --> 00:25:52,948
- Other possible causes?
- Infection.

392
00:25:53,018 --> 00:25:55,248
Start him on antibiotics. What else?

393
00:25:55,320 --> 00:25:58,756
Come on! Come on!
L-I don't know.

394
00:25:58,824 --> 00:26:02,157
You're useless.
But at least you know it.

395
00:26:02,227 --> 00:26:04,787
Blood tests show elevated creatine kinase.

396
00:26:04,863 --> 00:26:06,990
- What does that tell you?
- The trauma diagnosis was right.

397
00:26:07,065 --> 00:26:09,625
- He takes it easy
for a few days, he'll be fine.
- You sure?

398
00:26:09,701 --> 00:26:13,137
- The elevated C.K. Rules out infection-
- You know what's worse than useless?

399
00:26:13,205 --> 00:26:16,299
Useless and oblivious.

400
00:26:16,375 --> 00:26:19,139
What are they missing?

401
00:26:19,211 --> 00:26:21,475
It's kind of hard to think
when you're in our face like-

402
00:26:21,547 --> 00:26:25,643
Yeah? You think it's gonna be easier
when you got a real patient really dying?

403
00:26:25,717 --> 00:26:27,651
What are you missing?

404
00:26:27,719 --> 00:26:29,914
[Cameron]
Muscle death.

405
00:26:33,091 --> 00:26:38,028
- Not your case.
- Nothing wrong with a consult.

406
00:26:38,096 --> 00:26:42,863
Dying muscle leaks myoglobin.
It's toxic to the kidneys.

407
00:26:42,935 --> 00:26:45,699
Brilliant.

408
00:26:45,771 --> 00:26:49,229
[House]
M.R.I. His leg.
See what's killing it.

409
00:26:56,248 --> 00:26:59,308
- Why is the girl getting the M.R.I.?
- Because the neck scan revealed nothing,

410
00:26:59,384 --> 00:27:03,320
and her doctor's way more obsessive
than she thinks she is.

411
00:27:03,388 --> 00:27:06,789
- But you said the guy needed the M.R.I.
- Because Dr. Cameron back there
said muscle death.

412
00:27:06,859 --> 00:27:10,158
Not one of you said it.
Not one of this guy's doctors said it.

413
00:27:10,229 --> 00:27:14,689
They gave him bed rest and antibiotics,
just like you guys would have.

414
00:27:14,766 --> 00:27:17,462
- Did he get better?
- No.

415
00:27:17,536 --> 00:27:20,300
- How long-
- Three days.

416
00:27:20,372 --> 00:27:24,536
It is in the nature of medicine
that you are gonna screw up.

417
00:27:24,610 --> 00:27:26,544
You are gonna kill someone.

418
00:27:26,612 --> 00:27:30,878
If you can't handle that reality,
pick another profession.

419
00:27:30,949 --> 00:27:34,544
Or finish medical school and teach.

420
00:27:34,620 --> 00:27:37,589
Three days before they thought
it might have been muscle death?

421
00:27:37,656 --> 00:27:42,150
No, three days before the patient
suggested it might have been muscle death.

422
00:27:45,464 --> 00:27:49,662
The M.R.I. Revealed an osteosarcoma.

423
00:27:49,735 --> 00:27:55,264
A cancerous tumor on your femur.
It needs to be removed surgically.

424
00:27:55,340 --> 00:27:58,104
With chemo, she has
an excellent chance of survival.

425
00:27:58,176 --> 00:28:03,705
But I have to warn you-
depending on how large the tumor is
and how ingrained it is,

426
00:28:06,184 --> 00:28:09,085
the surgeon may need
to amputate your leg.

427
00:28:10,923 --> 00:28:12,914
I'm sorry.

428
00:28:12,991 --> 00:28:15,755
[Sobbing]
It's okay.

429
00:28:21,066 --> 00:28:23,000
Dogs' mouths are pretty filthy,

430
00:28:23,068 --> 00:28:26,265
but they have natural antibodies
to fight off most of the stuff.

431
00:28:26,338 --> 00:28:28,863
We don't. That's why
dog bites can be so nasty.

432
00:28:28,941 --> 00:28:32,775
Lab tests of your dog's saliva
revealed a type of strep bacteria.

433
00:28:32,844 --> 00:28:36,007
It's commonly known
as the flesh-eating disease.

434
00:28:36,081 --> 00:28:39,539
We need to operate immediately
to remove the damaged tissue.

435
00:28:39,618 --> 00:28:42,416
We may need to remove the leg.

436
00:28:50,495 --> 00:28:53,760
The M.R.I. Revealed a problem.
No kidding.

437
00:28:53,832 --> 00:28:56,824
I'm sorry none of your doctors
found it earlier.

438
00:28:56,902 --> 00:29:00,998
I am personally gonna oversee
your treatment from now on.
You're gonna cut me open, aren't you?

439
00:29:03,508 --> 00:29:05,908
We may need to remove the leg.

440
00:29:16,188 --> 00:29:22,024
His M.R.I. Showed that the leg pain
wasn't caused by the self-injection.

441
00:29:22,094 --> 00:29:24,585
It wasn't caused by an infection.

442
00:29:26,064 --> 00:29:29,397
It was an aneurysm that clotted...

443
00:29:29,468 --> 00:29:32,699
leading to an infarction.

444
00:29:32,771 --> 00:29:35,763
My God, you were right.
It's House.

445
00:29:37,309 --> 00:29:39,800
[Cuddy]
We have to do the surgery.

446
00:29:39,878 --> 00:29:43,143
The necrotic tissue has to be removed.
If there's too much-

447
00:29:43,215 --> 00:29:45,308
I don't care what you find.

448
00:29:45,384 --> 00:29:47,443
It may become necessary
in order to save your life.

449
00:29:47,519 --> 00:29:50,147
I like my leg.

450
00:29:50,222 --> 00:29:53,157
I've had it for as long as I can remember.

451
00:29:53,225 --> 00:29:57,855
Greg, I love your leg
as much as you do.
They're not cutting it off.

452
00:30:00,499 --> 00:30:03,434
The patient made the right choice.

453
00:30:03,502 --> 00:30:06,767
Tell a surgeon that
it's okay to cut a leg off,

454
00:30:06,838 --> 00:30:09,636
and he's gonna spend the night
polishing his good hacksaw.

455
00:30:09,708 --> 00:30:12,973
Right. Surgeons could care less
about saving limbs.

456
00:30:13,045 --> 00:30:17,141
Of course they care about their patients.
They just care about themselves more,

457
00:30:17,215 --> 00:30:19,979
which is not an unreasonable position.

458
00:30:20,052 --> 00:30:22,179
Trying to maximize the tissue you save...

459
00:30:22,254 --> 00:30:25,314
also maximizes the chances
of something going wrong,

460
00:30:25,390 --> 00:30:29,121
which means you've gotta be extra careful,
which is such a pain in the ass.

461
00:30:31,196 --> 00:30:34,825
Amazing advances have been made.
Kids with prosthetic legs...

462
00:30:34,900 --> 00:30:37,095
are running the hundred-meter dash
in 12 seconds.

463
00:30:37,169 --> 00:30:39,637
Yeah, they're just not as pretty.

464
00:30:40,939 --> 00:30:43,806
Do a bypass.
Restore the circulation.

465
00:30:43,875 --> 00:30:46,275
- Amputation is safer.
- For you or me?

466
00:30:46,344 --> 00:30:50,144
- The blockage of blood flow-
- Four-day blockage.

467
00:30:50,215 --> 00:30:52,274
Yes. It caused muscle cell death.

468
00:30:52,350 --> 00:30:54,944
When those cells die,
they release cytokines and potassium.

469
00:30:55,020 --> 00:30:58,046
And if you restore the blood flow
instead of just lopping it all off,

470
00:30:58,123 --> 00:31:00,455
then all that crap gets washed
back into my system.

471
00:31:00,525 --> 00:31:04,359
The cytokines could cause organ failure.
The potassium could cause cardiac arrest.

472
00:31:04,429 --> 00:31:07,296
On the other hand,
I may just get the use of my leg back.

473
00:31:08,834 --> 00:31:11,302
The postoperative pain alone will-
I'll get through it.

474
00:31:11,369 --> 00:31:15,135
I understand the risks. You're
in the clear. Go schedule an O.R.

475
00:31:22,013 --> 00:31:26,712
- God, you're an idiot.
- I think I'm more of a jerk.

476
00:31:26,785 --> 00:31:29,345
I'm not being glib,
and I'm not being cute.

477
00:31:29,421 --> 00:31:32,254
I don't want you to kill yourself.

478
00:31:32,324 --> 00:31:34,417
I'm not gonna die.

479
00:31:34,493 --> 00:31:37,690
Oh, I feel completely reassured.

480
00:32:06,491 --> 00:32:08,686
More suction.

481
00:32:10,896 --> 00:32:13,990
[Tube Sucking]

482
00:32:18,103 --> 00:32:21,698
[Instrument Whirring]

483
00:32:34,553 --> 00:32:37,147
[Indistinct Chattering]

484
00:32:52,804 --> 00:32:54,738
Inserting now.

485
00:33:12,791 --> 00:33:15,692
[Man Screaming]

486
00:33:18,396 --> 00:33:22,696
[Gasping]
They gotta up the morphine.

487
00:33:22,767 --> 00:33:28,000
- Doctors say they can't.
- Doctors recommended
bed rest and antibiotics.

488
00:33:28,073 --> 00:33:31,440
- They screwed up.
It doesn't mean they're wrong this time.
- Sure doesn't mean they're right.

489
00:33:31,509 --> 00:33:33,807
- Morphine will kill you.
- I can handle it.

490
00:33:33,878 --> 00:33:37,678
- You're in pain.
You're not thinking right.
- That's why I need the damn morphine!

491
00:33:37,749 --> 00:33:40,274
- I'll talk to them.
- [Panting]

492
00:33:42,787 --> 00:33:45,847
Oh, my God. How much longer
is the pain gonna last?

493
00:33:45,924 --> 00:33:48,324
It depends on how much
muscle cell death there was.

494
00:33:48,393 --> 00:33:52,056
He could be right.
He could come out of this
with almost full use of his leg.

495
00:33:52,130 --> 00:33:53,358
Or?

496
00:33:53,431 --> 00:33:56,366
He could be in pain
for the rest of his life.

497
00:33:59,437 --> 00:34:02,964
There's a third option...
surgically.

498
00:34:03,041 --> 00:34:06,010
A middle ground between
what we did and amputation.

499
00:34:09,714 --> 00:34:12,581
He's not big on middle ground.

500
00:34:12,651 --> 00:34:14,915
Yeah.

501
00:34:18,556 --> 00:34:21,184
[Machine Beeping]

502
00:34:38,843 --> 00:34:42,711
Nurse? Nurse!

503
00:34:42,781 --> 00:34:45,841
- I need more calcium gluconate.
- You just had five m.I.'s.

504
00:34:45,917 --> 00:34:49,011
The Q. R.S. Is getting wider.
My potassium is rising.

505
00:34:49,087 --> 00:34:51,282
- I'll talk to your doctor.
- Well, you better make it fast,

506
00:34:51,356 --> 00:34:53,381
'cause I'm about to go
into cardiac arrest.

507
00:34:53,458 --> 00:34:57,895
You give me the dose, or I go
into wide complex tachycardia.

508
00:34:57,962 --> 00:34:59,759
- I could get in trouble-
- Listen, it's not a narcotic!

509
00:34:59,831 --> 00:35:03,096
I'm not looking for a buzz.
You got about 20 seconds.

510
00:35:03,168 --> 00:35:06,604
- [Panting]
- [Hitting Buttons]

511
00:35:06,671 --> 00:35:09,401
[Machine Beeping]

512
00:35:09,474 --> 00:35:12,637
I was wrong.

513
00:35:12,711 --> 00:35:15,805
[Beeping Continues]

514
00:35:17,615 --> 00:35:20,641
What do you got?
Wide complex tachycardia.

515
00:35:20,719 --> 00:35:22,152
You diagnosed-
He did.

516
00:35:22,220 --> 00:35:23,915
Defibrillator.
Paddles.

517
00:35:25,023 --> 00:35:26,456
Charge.
Clear.

518
00:35:26,524 --> 00:35:29,288
[Steady Beeping]

519
00:35:31,863 --> 00:35:35,424
The patient was technically dead
for over a minute.

520
00:35:36,534 --> 00:35:38,593
[Dog Barking]

521
00:35:46,644 --> 00:35:49,636
[Barking Continues]

522
00:35:53,852 --> 00:35:57,083
[Crowd Applauding]

523
00:36:08,867 --> 00:36:11,893
[Machine Beeping]
He's back.

524
00:36:18,276 --> 00:36:20,676
[Wilson]
Do you think he was dead?

525
00:36:20,745 --> 00:36:22,542
Do you think
those experiences were real?

526
00:36:24,449 --> 00:36:28,852
Define "real."
They were real experiences.

527
00:36:28,920 --> 00:36:32,151
What they meant-

528
00:36:32,223 --> 00:36:35,522
Personally, I choose to believe
that the white light people sometimes see,

529
00:36:35,593 --> 00:36:38,619
the visions this patient saw,

530
00:36:38,696 --> 00:36:42,655
they're all just chemical reactions
that take place while the brain shuts down.

531
00:36:44,269 --> 00:36:47,898
- You choose to believe that?
- There's no conclusive science.

532
00:36:47,972 --> 00:36:51,965
My choice has no practical
relevance to my life.

533
00:36:52,043 --> 00:36:54,477
I choose the outcome
I find more comforting.

534
00:36:54,546 --> 00:36:57,413
You find it more comforting
to believe that this is it?

535
00:36:58,550 --> 00:37:03,453
Lfind it more comforting to believe
that this... isn't simply a test.

536
00:37:07,725 --> 00:37:12,162
- [Panting]
- How bad is the pain right now?

537
00:37:12,230 --> 00:37:15,199
[Gasps]
It's bad.

538
00:37:15,266 --> 00:37:18,929
It's not getting any better.
If you were right,
the pain would be subsiding.

539
00:37:19,003 --> 00:37:21,801
You'd be getting better.
It's just taking longer.

540
00:37:21,873 --> 00:37:23,841
No, it's not.

541
00:37:23,908 --> 00:37:26,775
We've gotta let 'em
cut the leg off.

542
00:37:29,714 --> 00:37:34,014
It's my leg. It's my life.

543
00:37:34,085 --> 00:37:36,713
Would you give up your leg
to save my life?

544
00:37:36,788 --> 00:37:38,915
Of course I would.

545
00:37:38,990 --> 00:37:41,220
Why do you think your life
is worth less than mine?

546
00:37:43,428 --> 00:37:46,625
If this were any other patient,
what would you tell them to do?

547
00:37:51,402 --> 00:37:54,838
I would say it's their choice.
[Stammers]
Not a chance.

548
00:37:54,906 --> 00:37:58,899
You'd browbeat them
until they made the choice
you knew was right.

549
00:37:58,977 --> 00:38:02,936
You'd shove it in their face
that it's just a damn leg.

550
00:38:04,649 --> 00:38:07,413
You don't think you deserve to live?

551
00:38:07,485 --> 00:38:10,648
You don't think you deserve to be happy?

552
00:38:12,624 --> 00:38:15,115
Now let them cut off your leg.

553
00:38:15,193 --> 00:38:20,893
[Panting, Stammering]
I can't. I can't.

554
00:38:20,965 --> 00:38:22,899
I'm sorry.

555
00:38:30,875 --> 00:38:33,844
The pain alone is gonna kill you.

556
00:38:33,912 --> 00:38:37,313
I know. I know.

557
00:38:40,985 --> 00:38:43,954
I need you to talk to the doctor.

558
00:38:52,163 --> 00:38:54,097
He change his mind?

559
00:38:54,165 --> 00:38:55,826
No.

560
00:38:57,769 --> 00:39:00,431
He's asked to be put
in a chemically induced coma...

561
00:39:00,505 --> 00:39:03,338
so that he can sleep through
the worst part of the pain.

562
00:39:05,310 --> 00:39:08,108
We can do that.

563
00:39:09,480 --> 00:39:12,278
What happens after he's in the coma?

564
00:39:12,350 --> 00:39:14,841
We'll obviously monitor
his condition closely,

565
00:39:14,919 --> 00:39:18,878
and if he can get through
the next 48 hours without
another cardiac incident-

566
00:39:18,957 --> 00:39:23,826
I meant, I'm his health care proxy.
I get to make medical decisions for him
if he's not able to.

567
00:39:26,598 --> 00:39:29,396
You should talk to him
about what he wants to do.

568
00:39:29,467 --> 00:39:32,834
I know what he wants.
But if he's out, it's my call, right?

569
00:39:40,979 --> 00:39:43,709
[Machine Beeping]

570
00:39:50,054 --> 00:39:52,147
You'll be out in less than a minute.

571
00:39:56,894 --> 00:39:58,828
Thank you.

572
00:40:03,001 --> 00:40:06,266
- Hey.
- Hey.

573
00:40:06,337 --> 00:40:08,237
I'll see you when I wake up.

574
00:40:09,974 --> 00:40:14,035
- We'll go golfing.
- [Laughs]

575
00:40:16,180 --> 00:40:18,205
I love you.

576
00:40:20,118 --> 00:40:22,052
I love you too.

577
00:40:25,356 --> 00:40:27,290
I'm sorry.

578
00:40:31,162 --> 00:40:33,357
You've got nothing to be sorry about.

579
00:40:39,804 --> 00:40:42,272
[Sighs]

580
00:40:52,016 --> 00:40:55,076
The middle ground you were talking about-

581
00:40:55,153 --> 00:40:57,621
We go in, take out the dead muscle.

582
00:40:57,689 --> 00:41:00,157
There's still some risk
of reperfusion injury, but-

583
00:41:00,224 --> 00:41:02,385
Give me the forms you need signed.

584
00:41:06,364 --> 00:41:08,855
You're saving his life.

585
00:41:08,933 --> 00:41:11,834
He won't see it that way.

586
00:41:17,341 --> 00:41:19,969
[Door Slides Opens]

587
00:41:20,044 --> 00:41:23,411
[Beeping Continues]

588
00:41:30,254 --> 00:41:34,384
Because of the extent
of the muscle removed,

589
00:41:34,459 --> 00:41:38,623
the utility of the patient's leg
was severely compromised.

590
00:41:38,696 --> 00:41:42,496
Because of the time delay
in making the diagnosis,

591
00:41:42,567 --> 00:41:45,798
the patient continues
to experience chronic pain.

592
00:41:52,543 --> 00:41:54,534
[Female Student]
She had no right to do that.

593
00:41:54,612 --> 00:41:57,012
- She had the proxy.
- She knew he didn't want the surgery.

594
00:41:57,081 --> 00:41:59,811
- She saved his life.
- We don't know that.
Maybe he would've been fine.

595
00:41:59,884 --> 00:42:03,445
- It doesn't matter. It's the patient's call.
- The patient's an idiot.

596
00:42:05,123 --> 00:42:07,489
They usually are.

597
00:42:07,558 --> 00:42:11,358
Do you have a buzzer or something?
What time does this class end?

598
00:42:11,429 --> 00:42:13,693
Twenty minutes ago.

599
00:42:19,137 --> 00:42:21,503
I'm not doing this again.

600
00:42:21,572 --> 00:42:25,508
And this guy is not the world's
greatest dad. Not even ranked.

601
00:42:25,576 --> 00:42:30,309
Who the hell lets their kids
play with lead-based paint?
That's why he's always sick.

602
00:42:30,381 --> 00:42:33,873
Find him some plastic cups,
and the class is all his again.

603
00:42:53,638 --> 00:42:56,607
[Cell Phone Beeping]

604
00:43:02,013 --> 00:43:04,311
Stacy, it's Greg.

605
00:43:04,382 --> 00:43:08,079
I've got an opening in my calendar
for 10:00 tomorrow morning.

606
00:43:08,152 --> 00:43:10,746
Make sure your husband isn't late.

607
00:43:12,023 --> 00:43:13,957
[Beeps]

608
00:43:58,135 --> 00:43:59,397
That's some bad hat, Harry.

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
<font color="#ffff00" size=14>www.tvsubtitles.net</font>
